{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-portability","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds shell helpers and rewrites awk/bash flows where Bash 3.2, BSD awk, quoting, temp files, and trap behavior are high-risk.","prompt_body":"Investigate the shell changes for Bash 3.2 and BSD/macOS portability, especially awk usage, process substitution assumptions, regex syntax, quoting, temp-file handling, and cleanup traps. Pay close attention to normalize-oos-block-header.sh, oos-serialize.sh, tally-code-votes.sh, emit-tally.sh, and review-and-fix.sh interactions. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"oos-pipeline","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The fix depends on preserving accepted OOS blocks across multiple scripts without overwrites, truncation, duplication, or missed mirrors.","prompt_body":"Trace accepted out-of-scope findings from tally-code-votes.sh through emit-tally.sh, review-and-fix.sh, accumulated artifacts, and implement disposition gates. Look for integration failures where counts, file aliases, missing files, mirrors, or round-to-round accumulation could drop, duplicate, or misclassify OOS blocks. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"oos-contract","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The change introduces a precise header-normalization and security-classification contract that could fail on subtle block formats.","prompt_body":"Examine whether OOS block parsing and normalization correctly handle tagged FINDING headers, bare scope-drift FINDING headers, existing OOS headers, multi-line bodies, rejected vote tallies, and security-held blocks. Verify that only intended headers are normalized or counted and that security-tagged items cannot leak into public accepted-OOS sinks. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"parity","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff changes both awk and Python OOS counters, so semantic drift between runtime and Python paths is a focused risk.","prompt_body":"Compare the awk and Python OOS counting implementations for semantic parity across block starts, legacy tagged headers, bare FINDING headers, security exclusion, multiline markdown, and file-level guards. Check whether tests exercise the same contract in both implementations and whether any divergence could affect ship-pr, disposition gates, or Python reporting paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"harness-wiring","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan relies heavily on regression harness coverage and Makefile shard wiring to catch future OOS pipeline regressions.","prompt_body":"Review the added and modified test harnesses plus Makefile wiring to ensure the new regression cases actually run under the intended lint and CI paths. Look for false-positive tests, missing assertions, brittle grep patterns, shard omissions, or fixtures that do not reproduce the production overwrite and skipped-routing paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
